							{Object.entries(skills).map(([label, tags]) => (
								<div class="skill-group">
									<span class="skill-label">{label}</span>
									<div class="skill-tags">
										{tags.map(t => <span class="tag">{t}</span>)}
									</div>
								</div>
							))}
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- References -->
					<div class="subsection">
						<h3 class="subsection-heading">Academic References</h3>
						<div class="refs-grid">
							<div class="ref-card">
								<p class="ref-name">Prof. Giovanni Ravazzani</p>
								<p class="ref-role">Thesis Supervisor · DICA, Politecnico di Milano</p>
								<a href="mailto:giovanni.ravazzani@polimi.it">giovanni.ravazzani@polimi.it</a>
							</div>
							<div class="ref-card">
								<p class="ref-name">Prof. Marco Gianinetto</p>
								<p class="ref-role">Remote Sensing · DICA, Politecnico di Milano</p>
								<a href="mailto:marco.gianinetto@polimi.it">marco.gianinetto@polimi.it</a>
							</div>
							<div class="ref-card">
								<p class="ref-name">Prof. Ilenia Epifani</p>
								<p class="ref-role">Statistical Models &amp; Stochastic Processes · DMAT, Politecnico di Milano</p>
								<a href="mailto:ilenia.epifani@polimi.it">ilenia.epifani@polimi.it</a>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<!-- ── SECTION 2: RELEVANT ACADEMIC WORK ── -->
				<section class="page-section">
					<h2 class="section-heading">Relevant Academic Work</h2>

					<!-- FEATURED PROJECT: 11 time series -->
					<div class="featured-project">

						<div class="fp-header">
							<span class="fp-badge">Featured project</span>
							<h3 class="fp-title">
								Statistical Validation of 11 Paleoclimate Time Series
							</h3>
							<p class="fp-subtitle">
								Identifying Climate Epochs Across 2,500 Years of European Temperature Reconstructions
							</p>
							<div class="fp-meta">
								<span>Politecnico di Milano · Statistical Models and Stochastic Processes · 2023</span>
								<span>R · Bland-Altman · Segmented regression · Shapiro-Wilk · Paired t-test</span>
							</div>
						</div>

						<p class="fp-overview">
							Eleven published paleoclimate reconstructions of European temperatures, spanning 500 BC to 2018 AD
							and built on four independent proxy families — tree-ring dendrochronology, Grape Harvest Dates,
							lake sediment chironomids, and historical/documentary indices — were assembled into a single
							validated multi-method dataset. The central question was whether these methods agree on the timing
							and magnitude of known climate epochs, and where they systematically diverge.
						</p>

						<img
							src="/portfolio/img/03-statistical/01_timeseries_overview.png"
							alt="Overview of all 11 paleoclimate reconstruction time series"
							class="fp-img-full"
						/>

						<div class="steps-block">
							<h4 class="steps-heading">Analytical Pipeline</h4>
							<div class="steps-grid">
								<div class="step">
									<span class="step-num">1</span>
									<div class="step-body">
										<strong>Dataset assembly and pre-processing</strong>
										<p>
											Standardised all 11 series to a common reference period; applied outlier removal
											to isolate climate signal from local noise; addressed non-uniform temporal
											coverage and missing data patterns across proxy families spanning 2,500 years.
										</p>
									</div>
								</div>
								<div class="step">
									<span class="step-num">2</span>
									<div class="step-body">
										<strong>Pairwise comparison — Bland-Altman analysis</strong>
										<p>
											Quantified systematic bias and limits of agreement between every pair of
											reconstruction methods. Normality of pairwise differences verified with
											Shapiro-Wilk tests (p &gt; 0.5 across all pairs) before running paired t-tests.
										</p>
									</div>
								</div>
								<div class="step">
									<span class="step-num">3</span>
									<div class="step-body">
										<strong>Regime-change / climate epoch detection</strong>
										<p>
											Fitted segmented linear regression with automatic breakpoint detection on each
											series to test whether all methods captured the same climate epochs: the Little
											Ice Age (1645–1715 and 1816–1850) and the Medieval Warm Period.
										</p>
									</div>
								</div>
								<div class="step">
									<span class="step-num">4</span>
									<div class="step-body">
										<strong>Cross-proxy reconciliation</strong>
										<p>
											Identified which proxy families agreed and explained divergences
											mechanistically — dendrochronology underestimates cold extremes relative to
											hemispheric reconstructions (Mann et al. 1999), a non-trivial finding for any
											user combining the two proxy families in downstream analysis.
										</p>
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="results-row">
							<div class="result-block">
								<img
									src="/portfolio/img/03-statistical/02_bland_altman.jpg"
									alt="Bland-Altman pairwise bias analysis between reconstruction methods"
									class="result-img"
								/>
								<p class="img-caption">Bland-Altman pairwise bias matrix across reconstruction methods</p>
							</div>
							<div class="result-block">
								<img
									src="/portfolio/img/03-statistical/03_regime_change.png"
									alt="Regime change detection via segmented linear regression"
									class="result-img"
								/>
								<p class="img-caption">Segmented regression breakpoint detection on four representative methods</p>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="findings-box">
							<h4 class="findings-heading">Key Findings</h4>
							<ul class="findings-list">
								<li>
									Dendrochronology methods systematically underestimate cold extremes compared to
									hemispheric reconstructions — a bias that propagates into any downstream analysis
									that mixes the two proxy families without correction.
								</li>
								<li>
									Only 1 of 4 tested methods captured both Little Ice Age sub-periods (1645–1715 and
									1816–1850); chironomid-based methods detect the broad regime shift but miss the
									sharper 1816–1850 sub-event.
								</li>
								<li>
									Shapiro-Wilk p-values consistently above 0.5 confirmed normality of pairwise
									differences, validating the use of parametric tests across the full comparison matrix.
								</li>
								<li>
									Produced a reproducible R workflow — standardisation → Bland-Altman →
									breakpoint detection → synthesis — directly applicable to any multi-source
									environmental time series, including NWP ensemble comparison or multi-station
									hydrometric datasets.
								</li>
							</ul>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- SECONDARY PROJECT: Po River Thesis -->
					<div class="secondary-project">

						<div class="sp-header">
							<span class="sp-badge">M.Sc. Thesis</span>
							<h3 class="sp-title">
								Impact of Agricultural Irrigation on the Hydrologic Regime of the Po River Basin
							</h3>
							<p class="sp-meta">
								Politecnico di Milano · Supervisor: Prof. Giovanni Ravazzani · 2024 ·
								FEST (Fortran) · Python · R · QGIS · MERIDA reanalysis
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="sp-body">
							<div class="sp-text">
								<p>
									Built and calibrated the <strong>FEST process-based hydrological model</strong> across
									the Po River Basin, integrating 10 years of spatially distributed meteorological,
									hydrological, and land-use data. Conducted a structured sensitivity and uncertainty
									analysis across five parameter families (deep percolation factor, SCS Curve Number,
									hydraulic conductivity, river-aquifer interaction coefficients, channel routing), then
									designed a counterfactual "no-irrigation" scenario to isolate the irrigation signal
									from natural climatic variability.
								</p>
								<p>
									The central methodological challenge — inferring the individual contributions of
									surface runoff, interflow, and deep percolation from aggregate streamflow observations —
									is structurally analogous to the isotopic water-source separation problem: in both
									cases, multiple sub-processes must be disentangled from an integrated signal using
									multi-source observations and uncertainty-aware calibration.
								</p>
								<ul class="sp-results">
									<li>
										Irrigation reduces cumulative river volumes by <strong>15% during the
										irrigation season</strong> (April–September), with measurable amplification of
										summer low-flow stress.
									</li>
									<li>
										Validated across 6 Po mainstem stations: mean NSE 0.58, mean NRMSE 0.50,
										∆% on cumulative volume between 1% and 11%, stable between calibration and
										validation periods.
									</li>
									<li>
										Sensitivity analysis identified deep percolation factor and SCS Curve Number as
										dominant discharge controls; hydraulic conductivity governs aquifer dynamics —
										a non-obvious result relevant to parameter prioritisation in future calibration.
									</li>
								</ul>
							</div>
							<div class="sp-imgs">
								<img
									src="/portfolio/img/01-po/01_po_basin_map.png"
									alt="Po basin sub-catchments and measurement stations"
									class="sp-img"
								/>
								<img
									src="/portfolio/img/01-po/03_sensitivity_tornado.png"
									alt="Tornado sensitivity diagram for FEST model parameters"
									class="sp-img"
								/>
							</div>
						</div>

					</div>
				</section>

			</div>
		</main>

		<ContactCTA />
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

